 "drivingQuestion": "How can a price be set on a vat of olives under Rav's view?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "R' Ḥiyya's reading plus local custom",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_metzia.74a.11",
   "daf": "74a",
   "sentenceId": 10,
   "he": "וְעַל הַמַּעֲטָן שֶׁל זֵיתִים. וְהָא מְחוּסָּר מִכְמַר וְעַיּוֹלֵי לְבֵי דַפֵּי לְמֵידַשׁ וּלְמִנְגַּד! תָּנֵי רַבִּי חִיָּיא: עַל כּוֹמֶר שֶׁל זֵיתִים. הָא אִיכָּא תְּלָת! בְּאַתְרָא דְּהָהוּא דְּזָבֵין הָהוּא (מְנַגֵּיד).",
   "en": "The mishna teaches that one may set a price «for a vat of olives.» Based on this, the Gemara challenges the opinion of Rav: «But» there are still several actions that are «lacking: Warming» the olives in a stack, «bringing» the olives «to the olive press, pressing» them, «and drawing» the oil out into the pit where it is stored. The Gemara answers: «Rabbi Ḥiyya teaches» a baraita with a different version of the statement, which reads: «For a stack of olives» that has already been warmed. The Gemara challenges: «But there are three» actions that are lacking: Bringing the olives to the olive press, pressing them, and drawing the oil. The Gemara explains: The mishna is discussing «a place where» the local custom is that «the one who purchases» the olives is «the one who draws» the oil."
  }
 ],
